Settings, in the Claude Code shape:

```json
{
  "permissions": {
    "allow": ["Bash(make test)", "Bash(npm run lint)", "Bash(npm run typecheck)", "Edit(src/**)"],
    "ask": ["Bash(git push:*)"],
    "deny": ["Read(./.env)"]
  },
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      { "matcher": "Bash|Edit|Write", "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "./gate.sh hook" }] }
    ]
  }
}
```

The allow list holds the test, lint, and typecheck commands because they are safe and run many times an hour; asking each time would train Omar to approve without reading. `Edit(src/**)` keeps feature work flowing. `Read(./.env)` is denied, not asked, because secrets never belong in the context and there is no case where Quill needs them. `git push` asks so a person sees the branch.

The hook covers Bash, Edit, and Write so a shell command cannot reach a path the Edit rules fence off. Inside `gate.sh hook`, the email rule reads `tool_input.file_path`, and if it starts with `src/email/` it logs an ask and prints `{"hookSpecificOutput":{"hookEventName":"PreToolUse","permissionDecision":"ask","permissionDecisionReason":"Edits under src/email/ need Priya. See docs/email.md."}}` with exit 0. The reason names Priya and the runbook, so Omar knows who to ask and what to read while the action is paused. Every branch of the hook, including the default allow at the bottom, calls `./gate.sh log <decision> <rule> <tool> <input>`, which appends one JSON line with a UTC timestamp to `.gates/log.jsonl`. Allows are logged too, so a missing line cannot be mistaken for an allow. When Priya asks whether Quill touched email, the answer is a grep over that file.
